g for a few days the pump  was a little tricky to use but I was  lucky enough to be able to afford that  pump I thought I had figured out how to  assemble it but I didn't because when I  started pouring water into it it went  everywhere I could not for the life of  me figure out how the [ __ ] to use the  pump and spray this doesn't feel very  self-explanatory I think I might just  dump a bottle of water on my head today  is day two I have my towel my mini soap  and all my clothes in my backpack and  today I'm going to be shivering at the  gym the gym was the best because you  know at least I have access to the  running water so I stayed in there for  like 15 minutes before I felt like I had  to leave so I just started doing the  dishes and I realized that I can't  actually rinse them because I can't send  the top on I just left my friend's  apartment after borrowing her shower and  it was fantastic it was also  uncomfortable to do all the things you  do in a bathroom which are kind of  intimate and private in someone else's  bathroom like you just feel naked and  afraid  so the packed bag with my towel and  toiletries  today I'm going to shower at work it's  not the first time so this shouldn't be  too odd but the thought of having to do  it every day would be pretty  uncomfortable I don't think I had any  issue asking for help once her friend I  could see how that would take a big  emotional toll relying on other people  to provide you running water yeah that  would suck I'm so thirsty day three we  jumped in and out of the shower really  quickly apparently that's a thing that  people do in this situation I ended up  just like cleaning myself with baby  wipes a day really I tried to take the  water droplets and like rub them on  myself with the towel and like scrub  myself with the towel I felt really  sticky after because like I still felt  the need to moisturize because I really  dry skin it's time for the operation  baby wax day 4 was probably the easiest  day to clean myself and like the least  emotionally totally baby wipes are great  I might do that more often wipe myself  down with scented baby wipes today not  the business they smell great they don't  do anything for you I probably used up  half the package I was just kind of like  scrubbing under my arms I'm not gonna  want this everywhere if you know what I  mean  the thing that gets me the most is on  drones after having to do this and they  after a couple days this still feels  like I'm lucky to have this  like a baby all day I did not use paper  plates at all but I also ended up  putting off washing my dishes because  it's so [ __ ] hard when you don't have  running water really the hottest part  was just the emotional strain of knowing  that this would be over in a week and  that there are hundreds of thousands of  people in the world you have to live  this every single day I became really  really aware of how much water I was  using I would never let water run down  the drain without thinking to myself  okay is there anything else I can rinse  off with this I noticed when it's  raining there's these repeat offender  homes and they all have their sprinklers  running while it's raining that's water  someone else in Flint or anywhere else  in the world where they don't get access  to water could be using but you're just  wasting it I feel like we don't fully  understand that until you kind of cut  yourself off from what you have you just  don't realize how bad it is for other  people I wasn't able to shower in the  morning so I showered at night with the  boiled water because it took so long to  cool and I had to boil so much of it to  actually use it it was definitely an  exercise in patience the head got down I  don't know how you could do any of this  with with wrong hair even short hair I  haven't washed my hair at all in the  past few days I've lived in really all  areas of the Middle East where we didn't  have running water or when we did it  wasn't clean that was only for a few  months at a time but to leave that  everyday to have a baby and to not be  able to bathe it or long hair that you  need to wash and it can't get in your  eyes or mouth every single day it was  easier than I expected in some ways and  also surprised me at how hard it was in  other ways I was able to connect a  little bit more to that situation  because you can read an article but it's  not the same as going through it we can  recreate not having running water but we  can't recreate the emotional experience  of being forced to not have running  water and not knowing when you're going  to get running water back what I got out  of this was it taught me how much of a  necessity running water is and that it's  not really something that can get ever  be up for debate the biggest thing about  this video is that this could easily  come across as poverty tourism I don't  want this to be seen as some  kind of voyeuristic entertainment from  other people's suffering it's more just  to get the word out there and to  hopefully showcase that it's ridiculous  that this is happening in developed  countries but it's ridiculous that it's  happening full-stop the least I can do  especially after doing this experiment  is make a little noise about it we all  kind of band together and like make it a  big deal maybe something will happen  faster because there'll be more tension  on it there is a lot of information out  there if you're willing to look for it  EPA is a good place to start the Natural  Resources Defense Council  it's another excellent organization  that's very focused on this earth  justice places like that being mad or  sad about any problem is obviously never  enough it's actually about standing up  and doing something and hopefully this  video gets that message across  [Music]

